nding the inequalities or at least  attempting to put policies and laws in  place to end the Equality inequalities  that were the fiber of apartheid and to  take off the books all of the the whole  sort of legal apparatus that kept  apartheid going the rewriting of a  constitution literally the the rewriting  of the almost the entire legal Canon the  changing of policies from education to  the economy to health that was the South  African journey that I covered there  were other journeys as well one of them  that was most difficult was a journey of  Rwanda after its genocide of 1994 I  didn't cover that genocide because I  wasn't in that part of Africa at the  time I was actually in South Africa  covering the elections in 1994 when the  killing occurred in Rwanda that we all  now know as a genocide but I traveled to  Rwanda quite a bit in the later years 96  97 98 and was able to chronicle a bit  and witness some of that country  attempting to put itself back together  after having lost up to 800,000 people  in the genocide and that was a  particular kind of journey that was that  is still ongoing and for Rwanda will  probably go on for decades to come  because the genocide was such a  cataclysm the journeys in the book also  include Angola I was just talking to a  young man about Angola downstairs a  country in South West Africa that like a  few others on the continent was one of  the places that was heavily affected by  the cold war by the rivalry between the  US and the former Soviet Union and where  a war tore that country from even before  its independence from Portugal in 1975  and when I arrived a that war had been  continuing almost continuously through  all those years  with a bit of a break for an attempt at  elections in 92 but when I arrived and  first went to Angola in 1995 another  peace accord had been put in place and  the country was attempting to sort of  move forward from conflict it didn't  work and the war did go on and on and so  that journey of Angola was about a  country literally falling apart under  the weight of conflict and then it also  became the story of the country  journeying into sort of being a Bigfoot  in the region getting itself involved in  wars in the Khong in both congos the  Democratic Republic of Congo the  former's a year and the Congo Republic  which is also known as Congo Brazzaville  and so during my watch angola became  sort of a regional strategic kind of  power its military could be found in a  few places around the continent  the book is also about journeys in  places like Zimbabwe Zambia in Zimbabwe  as we know from the headlines the  government of Robert Mugabe was once  considered to be a beacon of stability  and an attempt at democracy but the the  attempt has sort of fallen apart in in  in no small measure because of Mugabe  his involvement in the war in Congo but  also just because the center of power in  that country is so deeply concentrated  in Mugabe himself and in his ruling  party that repression I think in that  kind of circumstance became inevitable  and so what we're seeing today on top of  that is looming famine in Zimbabwe and  other countries of the southern African  region in in part fuelled by the  policies of the government the journeys  were about countries as I've said but  the journeys as I described them were  also about my own journey as a  journalist I first went to South Africa  in 1990 after Nelson Mandela's release  from prison he had been in political  imprisonment for 28 years most of that  time spent on Robben Island and I went  back to South Africa in 1994 as part of  a team from our newspaper to cover the  elections when apartheid officially  ended  and when I returned in 1995 to take up  the posting as the bureau chief  I was completely infected with a certain  amount of optimism this momentum that  was South Africa the ending of apartheid  the rise of Mandela and his movement the  sense almost that anything was possible  so I had this very 