aking but spiritually were  incredibly rich and deep people and so  part of my journey was understanding  that the grounding for me in Africa were  the people and what that meant in terms  of how I viewed governments and how I  viewed policies from the outside world  was the sense that there needed to be  more programs and more aid and more  attention paid to improving the lives of  ordinary people and that's another  reason that is a reason why in the book  in my authors note which is at the very  beginning of the book I describe myself  as not an afro pessimist which is a  brand of person who just thinks you know  Africa is going or has gone to the dogs  and there's no reason to be concerned or  nor an afro optimists which is someone  who believes that everything is going to  turn out alright and and is very  defensive about the negatives in Africa  I called myself an afro realist and the  reason I did that is that I was up close  and right in the throes of some of the  chronic problems in Africa in some  African countries the problems of  governmental corruption of chaotic  bureaucracies of violence some of it  ethnic violence and you know so I can  not in good conscience claim that  everything is going to be alright  because I have seen how it is not and I  have seen reason to believe that there  will continue to be problems but as I  said before to me the the spirit of  Africa lies in the people who are legion  I mean ordinary people who continue to  live lives that are dignified to make  the best of their situations and many of  them put themselves at risk to try to  help their communities to be human  rights monitors etc and to me that's  that's the hope and the heart of Africa  and so I call myself a realist to  combine those two elements yes there are  problems but you know you can't turn  your back on this huge body of people  that are real people who are worthy  our attention and and respect I think so  the book has these different journeys  that I weave 